{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1436 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "ffd80c85773d2fb6fe5ce8f5cbc3f817cb64e1c698246b6471ac97dbcce3aa90" :edb/db-before-t 1435 :edb/db-after-t 1436 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534748 50 #inst "2026-10-08T05:46:58.274Z" 13194139534748 true] [17592186166558 1100 "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm  d t} + y + 6 = y^2.$ Which of the following is an equilibrium solution of this equation?\n\n- $y=-3$\n\n- $y=-2$\n\n- $y=-1$" 13194139534748 true] [17592186166558 1100 "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t} + y + 6 = y^2$. Which of the following is an equilibrium solution of this equation?\n\n1. $y=-3$\n2. $y=-2$\n3. $y=-1$" 13194139534748 false] [17592186166558 1145 "Given an autonomous differential equation\n\n$$\ny'(t) = f(y)\n$$\n\nif there exists a constant $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(c)=0\n$$\n\nthen $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nFirst, we write the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t} = y^2 - y - 6\n$$\n\nand we get that\n\n$$\nf(y) = y^2 - y - 6\n$$\n\nNow, let's check each proposed solution by substituting them into $f(y)$.\n\n- $y=-3$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(-3) = (-3)^2 - (-3) - 6 = 6 \\neq 0$. $\\quad \\color{red}{\\times}$\n- $y = -2$ is an equilibrium point. We have $f(-2) = (-2)^2 - (-2) - 6 = 0$.$\\quad \\color{green}{\\checkmark}$\n- $y= -1$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(-1) = (-1)^2 - (-1) - 6 = -4 \\neq 0$. $\\quad \\color{red}{\\times}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534748 false] [17592186166558 1145 "Given an autonomous differential equation $y'(t) = f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nFirst, we write the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm  d t} = y^2 - y - 6,\n$$\n\nand we get that $f(y) = y^2 - y - 6.$\n\nNow, let's check each proposed solution by substituting them into $f(y).$\n\n- $y=-3$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(-3) = (-3)^2 - (-3) - 6 = 6 \\neq 0.$  $\\quad \\color{red}{\\times}$\n\n- $y = -2$ is an equilibrium point. We have $f(-2) = (-2)^2 - (-2) - 6 = 0.$$\\quad  \\color{green}{\\checkmark}$\n\n- $y= -1$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(-1) = (-1)^2 - (-1) - 6 = -4 \\neq 0.$ $\\quad \\color{red}{\\times}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534748 true] [17592186166559 1100 "Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm  d x}= y^2+4y.$" 13194139534748 true] [17592186166559 1100 "Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}= y^2+4y$." 13194139534748 false] [17592186166559 1145 "Given an autonomous differential equation\n\n$$\ny'(x) = f(y)\n$$\n\nif there exists a constant $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(c)=0\n$$\n\nthen $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nSo, to find the equilibrium solutions of a differential equation, we must find all values of $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(c)=0\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have that\n\n$$\nf(y)= y^2+4y\n$$\n\nso we solve the equation\n\n$$\nf(c)=0\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(c) &= 0 \\\\\nc^{2} + 4c &= 0 \\\\\nc(c + 4) &= 0 \\\\\nc &= -4, 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y_1(x)=-4$ and $y_2(x)=0$ are the equilibrium solutions of the given differential equation." 13194139534748 false] [17592186166559 1145 "Given an autonomous differential equation $y'(x) = f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nSo, to find the equilibrium solutions of a differential equation, we must find all values of $c$ such that $f(c)=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)= y^2+4y,$ so we solve the equation $f(c)=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c) & =0 \\\\ {c}^{2}+4c & =0 \\\\ c(c+4) & =0 \\\\ c & =-4,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y_1(x)=-4$ and $y_2(x)=0$ are the equilibrium solutions of the given differential equation." 13194139534748 true] [17592186166560 1100 "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm  d x}= y^3-3y^2-6y+8.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y_1(x)=4$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation\n\n- The equation has $3$ distinct equilibrium solutions, $y_1(x), y_2(x),$ and $y_3(x)$\n\n- $y_1(x) + y_2(x)+y_3(x) =-3$" 13194139534748 true] [17592186166560 1100 "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}= y^3-3y^2-6y+8$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y_1(x)=4$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation\n2. The equation has $3$ distinct equilibrium solutions, $y_1(x), y_2(x)$, and $y_3(x)$\n3. $y_1(x) + y_2(x)+y_3(x) =-3$" 13194139534748 false] [17592186166560 1145 "Given an autonomous differential equation\n\n$$\ny'(x) = f(y)\n$$\n\nif there exists a constant $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(c)=0\n$$\n\nthen $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nIn our case, we have that\n\n$$\nf(y)=y^3-3y^2-6y+8\n$$\n\nLet's examine the statement one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have that $f(4)=4^3-3(4)^2-6(4)+8 = 0$.\n- Statement II is true. We know that $y=4$ is a solution to $f(y) = 0$, and so $(y-4)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(y)$. Dividing $f(y)$ by $(y-4)$ using synthetic division, we get\n$y^3$$y^2$$y^1$$y^0$$4$$1$$-3$$-6$$8$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$4$$4$$-8$$1$$1$$-2$$0$\nTherefore,\n$f(y)|= (y - 4)(y^{2} + y - 2); = (y - 4)(y - 1)(y + 2)$\nHence, $f(y)$ has three distinct equilibrium solutions,\n$y_1(x) =4, \\quad y_2(x) = 1, \\quad y_3(x) = -2$.\n- Statement III is false. We have $y_1(x)+y_2(x) + y_3(x) = 4+1+(-2) = 3$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534748 false] [17592186166560 1145 "Given an autonomous differential equation $y'(x) = f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)=y^3-3y^2-6y+8.$\n\nLet's examine the statement one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true.  We have that $f(4)=4^3-3(4)^2-6(4)+8 = 0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. We know that $y=4$ is a solution to $f(y) = 0,$ and so $(y-4)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(y).$ Dividing $f(y)$ by $(y-4)$ using synthetic division, we get\n\n|  | $y^3$ | $y^2$ | $y^1$ | $y^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $-3$ | $-6$ | $8$ |\n|  | $\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$ | $4$ | $4$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n\n \nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =(y-4)({y}^{2}+y-2) \\\\  & =(y-4)(y-1)(y+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(y)$ has three distinct equilibrium solutions, \n\n$$\ny_1(x) =4, \\quad y_2(x) = 1, \\quad y_3(x) = -2.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have $y_1(x)+y_2(x) + y_3(x) = 4+1+(-2) = 3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534748 true] [17592186166720 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307390 13194139534748 true] [17592186166720 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307397 13194139534748 true] [17592186166720 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307434 13194139534748 true] [17592186166720 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307441 13194139534748 true] [17592186166721 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307404 13194139534748 true] [17592186166721 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307411 13194139534748 true] [17592186166721 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307425 13194139534748 true] [17592186166721 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307429 13194139534748 true] [17592186166721 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307448 13194139534748 true] [17592186166722 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307418 13194139534748 true] [17592186166722 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307455 13194139534748 true] [17592186166722 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307462 13194139534748 true] [17592186166722 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307469 13194139534748 true] [17592186167327 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534748 true] [17592186167327 1100 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{y}^{2}=2y+{y}^{3}.$ Which of the following is an equilibrium solution of this equation?\n\n- $y=0$\n- $y=1$\n- $y=2$" 13194139534748 true] [17592186167327 1100 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} - y^{2} = 2y + y^{3}$. Which of the following is an equilibrium solution of this equation?\n\n1. $y = 0$\n2. $y = 1$\n3. $y = 2$" 13194139534748 false] [17592186167327 1145 "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(x)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nFirst, we write the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{3}+{y}^{2}+2y,\n$$\n\nand we get that $f(y)={y}^{3}+{y}^{2}+2y.$\n\nNow, let's check each proposed solution by substituting them into $f(y).$\n\n- $y=0$ is an equilibrium point. We have $f(0)={0}^{3}+{0}^{2}+2(0)=0.$$\\,✓$\n\n- $y=1$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(1)={1}^{3}+{1}^{2}+2(1)=4\\ne 0.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- $y=2$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(2)={2}^{3}+{2}^{2}+2(2)=16\\ne 0.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534748 true] [17592186167337 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534748 true] [17592186167337 1100 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{3}-7y+6.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)=1$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation\n- The equation has $3$ distinct equilibrium solutions, ${y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x),$ and ${y}_{3}(x)$\n- ${y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)+{y}_{3}(x)=0$" 13194139534748 true] [17592186167337 1100 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = y^{3} - 7y + 6$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y_{1}(x) = 1$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation\n2. The equation has $3$ distinct equilibrium solutions, $y_{1}(x), y_{2}(x)$, and $y_{3}(x)$\n3. $y_{1}(x) + y_{2}(x) + y_{3}(x) = 0$" 13194139534748 false] [17592186167337 1145 "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(x)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)={y}^{3}-7y+6.$\n\nLet's examine the statement one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true.  We have that $f(1)={1}^{3}-7(1)+6=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. We know that $y=1$ is a solution to $f(y)=0,$ and so $(y-1)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(y).$ Dividing $f(y)$ by $(y-1)$ using synthetic division, we get\n\n|  | ${y}^{3}$ | ${y}^{2}$ | ${y}^{1}$ | ${y}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-7$ | $6$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\n \nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =(y-1)({y}^{2}+y-6) \\\\  & =(y-1)(y-2)(y+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(y)$ has three distinct equilibrium solutions, \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=1,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=2,\\,{y}_{3}(x)=-3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have ${y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)+{y}_{3}(x)=1+2+(-3)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139534748 true] [17592186307390 1017 #edb/ref 17592186307391 13194139534748 true] [17592186307390 1089 "q-160419" 13194139534748 true] [17592186307390 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534748 true] [17592186307390 1100 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+\\pi .$ Which of the following is an equilibrium solution of this equation?\n\n- $y=0$\n- $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n- $y=\\pi $" 13194139534748 true] [17592186307390 1113 #uuid "539f5aea-e270-503e-8d41-9c9f4899cc73" 13194139534748 true] [17592186307390 1145 "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(t)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nFirst, we write the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y+\\pi ,\n$$\n\nand we get that $f(y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y+\\pi .$\n\nNow, let's check each proposed solution by substituting them into $f(y).$\n\n- $y=0$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(0)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-0+\\pi =\\pi \\ne 0.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi =1+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\ne 0.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- $y=\\pi $ is an equilibrium point. We have $f(\\pi )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\pi +\\pi =0-\\pi +\\pi =0.$$\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534748 true] [17592186307391 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307392 13194139534748 true] [17592186307391 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307393 13194139534748 true] [17592186307391 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307394 13194139534748 true] [17592186307391 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307395 13194139534748 true] [17592186307391 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307396 13194139534748 true] [17592186307391 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534748 true] [17592186307391 1041 "selection" 13194139534748 true] [17592186307391 1104 #edb/ref 17592186307395 13194139534748 true] [17592186307391 1110 #uuid "3ea9e8ce-313c-5433-8acc-cfa8fedbe7bc" 13194139534748 true] [17592186307392 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534748 true] [17592186307392 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534748 true] [17592186307392 1111 #uuid "9c3c2a28-0b6c-5ae8-a124-8a082c07c13a" 13194139534748 true] [17592186307393 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534748 true] [17592186307393 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534748 true] [17592186307393 1111 #uuid "ea527680-a145-5edf-8a6f-9b01dc8d7cba" 13194139534748 true] [17592186307394 1034 "I only" 13194139534748 true] [17592186307394 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534748 true] [17592186307394 1111 #uuid "98a5501b-5826-5c2d-a265-345b27c00842" 13194139534748 true] [17592186307395 1034 "III only" 13194139534748 true] [17592186307395 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534748 true] [17592186307395 1111 #uuid "c9e8ec0d-5c42-5c82-9bd5-a50232cf8f90" 13194139534748 true] [17592186307396 1034 "II only" 13194139534748 true] [17592186307396 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534748 true] [17592186307396 1111 #uuid "467c800a-fc1c-5411-8ecd-a08b8c80ec1d" 13194139534748 true] [17592186307397 1017 #edb/ref 17592186307398 13194139534748 true] [17592186307397 1089 "q-160422" 13194139534748 true] [17592186307397 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534748 true] [17592186307397 1100 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}=1.$ Which of the following is an equilibrium solution of this equation?\n\n- $y=-1$\n- $y=1$\n- $y=2$" 13194139534748 true] [17592186307397 1113 #uuid "480185ab-957a-5a1f-816b-f2661a017908" 13194139534748 true] [17592186307397 1145 "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(x)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nFirst, we write the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand we get that $f(y)=1-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}.$\n\nNow, let's check each proposed solution by substituting them into $f(y).$\n\n- $y=-1$ is an equilibrium point. We have $f(-1)=1-\\frac{1}{(-1)^{2}}=1-1=0.$$\\,✓$\n\n- $y=1$ is an equilibrium point. We have $f(1)=1-\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}}=1-1=0.$$\\,✓$\n\n- $y=2$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(2)=1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}}=\\frac{3}{4}\\ne 0.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534748 true] [17592186307398 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307399 13194139534748 true] [17592186307398 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307400 13194139534748 true] [17592186307398 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307401 13194139534748 true] [17592186307398 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307402 13194139534748 true] [17592186307398 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307403 13194139534748 true] [17592186307398 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534748 true] [17592186307398 1041 "selection" 13194139534748 true] [17592186307398 1104 #edb/ref 17592186307402 13194139534748 true] [17592186307398 1110 #uuid "4ebb1e5d-15a7-576b-b154-41aa9146354b" 13194139534748 true] [17592186307399 1034 "II and III