 1034 "7" 13194139534748 true] [17592186307428 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534748 true] [17592186307428 1111 #uuid "d42f5ca5-8dd5-57f0-af08-0c6c19254fbe" 13194139534748 true] [17592186307429 1017 #edb/ref 17592186307430 13194139534748 true] [17592186307429 1017 #edb/ref 17592186307432 13194139534748 true] [17592186307429 1089 "q-352884" 13194139534748 true] [17592186307429 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534748 true] [17592186307429 1100 "**In ascending order**, the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}={y}^{2}-2y-15$\nare\n\n${y}_{1}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}(t)=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139534748 true] [17592186307429 1113 #uuid "882b2bbe-3cb6-52dd-ac03-52548104d807" 13194139534748 true] [17592186307429 1145 "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(t)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nSo, to find the equilibrium solutions of a differential equation, we must find all values of $c$ such that $f(c)=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)={y}^{2}-2y-15,$ so we solve the equation $f(c)=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c) & =0 \\\\ {c}^{2}-2c-15 & =0 \\\\ (c-5)(c+3) & =0 \\\\ c & =-3,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, ${y}_{1}(t)=-3$ and ${y}_{2}(t)=5$ are the equilibrium solutions of the given differential equation." 13194139534748 true] [17592186307430 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307431 13194139534748 true] [17592186307430 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534748 true] [17592186307430 1041 "field-1" 13194139534748 true] [17592186307430 1104 #edb/ref 17592186307431 13194139534748 true] [17592186307430 1110 #uuid "71b5b1dc-22bd-5f6a-b98b-dc1103e26d48" 13194139534748 true] [17592186307431 1034 "-3" 13194139534748 true] [17592186307431 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534748 true] [17592186307431 1111 #uuid "c9aa54b8-3b41-5561-96cf-f3783de33add" 13194139534748 true] [17592186307432 1